  6. NAMESPACE INVARIANCE
  .......................
  Every javax name stays a javax name. The platform's removal of those
  modules is compensated by ADDING javax namespace artifacts, never by
  rewriting source to the successor namespace. Artifact eligibility was
  decided by inspecting each jar's PACKAGE PREFIXES rather than its
  coordinates, which cuts both ways and is why the rule is stated in terms
  of contents: an artifact merely NAMED for the successor namespace is
  rejected even when its own classes are javax prefixed, because carrying
  it would place a successor named jar on the classpath and duplicate a
  class set that is already present; and conversely a javax named release
  line is avoided where it drags in successor grouped transitives.
  Writing javax.xml.bind in this file is therefore CORRECT AND REQUIRED,
  and no successor namespace token appears anywhere in this file. This is
  the highest risk file in the archive for that gate, because its entire
  narrative concerns the removed XML binding API.

